    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-automations-e4-51d0be93",
      "x": 76,
      "y": 3470,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 420,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-31 (automations run lifecycle, slice E4): ONE new collection plus one new field.\nautomation_run_idempotency - _id IS sha256(JSON.stringify([automationId, runOwnerUserId, idempotencyKey])),\nrow {runId, at}. JSON-encoded, NOT separator-joined: an integration-provisioned automation's id is\n<integrationKey>-<templateKey> (integration-automations.ts), so the 'every component is a UUID' premise a\n`|` join needs was false (review hardening 5). Same deterministic-_id insert pattern as gateway_keys (§4.3.2: no unique indexes; the\nduplicate-key refusal from Store.insert IS the uniqueness primitive). Written BEFORE the run row so two\nsimultaneous POSTs race on a single-document insert instead of minting two runs. All three components\nmatter: without the automation two automations share a key, without the OWNER (the run's owner, which\nfor a super-admin-triggered run is the automation's owner, not the caller) one tenant's key collides\nwith another's. The caller's key is NEVER stored - only hashed into the _id. A duplicate always answers\nthe MAPPING, never a re-validated run: if the run is gone the honest answer is still 'this key was\nalready accepted', and GET /runs/:id 404s - re-creating would resurrect the double execution the key\nexists to prevent. The one rollback is a run that never started (the run insert threw), which deletes\nthe mapping so the key is not poisoned.\nautomation_runs.steps[] gains logTail {text, truncated} - a BOUNDED tail of what the step STREAMED,\n16 KB per step and 128 KB per run (shared RUN_LOG_STEP_MAX_CHARS / RUN_LOG_TOTAL_MAX_CHARS), rolling so\nthe END of a long command survives. Bounded TWICE: at write time by the engine's accumulator, and again\non read, because the other log source (the pre-existing StepRecord.output) carries up to 5 MB of\ncaptured stdout that must never become a 5 MB response. The tail is invisible to the run wire shape\n(RunStepRecord stays lean); GET /runs/:id/logs is its only reader.\nRETENTION IS UNBOUNDED, DELIBERATELY (review hardening 6): nothing reaps `at`. ~120 bytes per row, written\nONLY by clients that send a key (the dashboard sends none), so single-digit MB per million keyed creates.\nReaping is not free correctness - deleting a mapping RE-ARMS the key, so a later retry starts a second run;\nany future reaper needs a window longer than a client retries, stated as a risk, not a silent cleanup job.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-31 (automations run lifecycle, slice E4): ONE new collection plus one new field.\nautomation_run_idempotency - _id IS sha256(JSON.stringify([automationId, runOwnerUserId, idempotencyKey])),\nrow {runId, at}. JSON-encoded, NOT separator-joined: an integration-provisioned automation's id is\n<integrationKey>-<templateKey> (integration-automations.ts), so the 'every component is a UUID' premise a\n`|` join needs was false (review hardening 5). Same deterministic-_id insert pattern as gateway_keys (§4.3.2: no unique indexes; the\nduplicate-key refusal from Store.insert IS the uniqueness primitive). Written BEFORE the run row so two\nsimultaneous POSTs race on a single-document insert instead of minting two runs. All three components\nmatter: without the automation two automations share a key, without the OWNER (the run's owner, which\nfor a super-admin-triggered run is the automation's owner, not the caller) one tenant's key collides\nwith another's. The caller's key is NEVER stored - only hashed into the _id. A duplicate always answers\nthe MAPPING, never a re-validated run: if the run is gone the honest answer is still 'this key was\nalready accepted', and GET /runs/:id 404s - re-creating would resurrect the double execution the key\nexists to prevent. The one rollback is a run that never started (the run insert threw), which deletes\nthe mapping so the key is not poisoned.\nautomation_runs.steps[] gains logTail {text, truncated} - a BOUNDED tail of what the step STREAMED,\n16 KB per step and 128 KB per run (shared RUN_LOG_STEP_MAX_CHARS / RUN_LOG_TOTAL_MAX_CHARS), rolling so\nthe END of a long command survives. Bounded TWICE: at write time by the engine's accumulator, and again\non read, because the other log source (the pre-existing StepRecord.output) carries up to 5 MB of\ncaptured stdout that must never become a 5 MB response. The tail is invisible to the run wire shape\n(RunStepRecord stays lean); GET /runs/:id/logs is its only reader.\nRETENTION IS UNBOUNDED, DELIBERATELY (review hardening 6): nothing reaps `at`. ~120 bytes per row, written\nONLY by clients that send a key (the dashboard sends none), so single-digit MB per million keyed creates.\nReaping is not free correctness - deleting a mapping RE-ARMS the key, so a later retry starts a second run;\nany future reaper needs a window longer than a client retries, stated as a risk, not a silent cleanup job.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-31 (automations run lifecycle, slice E4): ONE new collection plus one new field.\nautomation_run_idempotency - _id IS sha256(JSON.stringify([automationId, runOwnerUserId, idempotencyKey])),\nrow {runId, at}. JSON-encoded, NOT separator-joined: an integration-provisioned automation's id is\n<integrationKey>-<templateKey> (integration-automations.ts), so the 'every component is a UUID' premise a\n`|` join needs was false (review hardening 5). Same deterministic-_id insert pattern as gateway_keys (§4.3.2: no unique indexes; the\nduplicate-key refusal from Store.insert IS the uniqueness primitive). Written BEFORE the run row so two\nsimultaneous POSTs race on a single-document insert instead of minting two runs. All three components\nmatter: without the automation two automations share a key, without the OWNER (the run's owner, which\nfor a super-admin-triggered run is the automation's owner, not the caller) one tenant's key collides\nwith another's. The caller's key is NEVER stored - only hashed into the _id. A duplicate always answers\nthe MAPPING, never a re-validated run: if the run is gone the honest answer is still 'this key was\nalready accepted', and GET /runs/:id 404s - re-creating would resurrect the double execution the key\nexists to prevent. The one rollback is a run that never started (the run insert threw), which deletes\nthe mapping so the key is not poisoned.\nautomation_runs.steps[] gains logTail {text, truncated} - a BOUNDED tail of what the step STREAMED,\n16 KB per step and 128 KB per run (shared RUN_LOG_STEP_MAX_CHARS / RUN_LOG_TOTAL_MAX_CHARS), rolling so\nthe END of a long command survives. Bounded TWICE: at write time by the engine's accumulator, and again\non read, because the other log source (the pre-existing StepRecord.output) carries up to 5 MB of\ncaptured stdout that must never become a 5 MB response. The tail is invisible to the run wire shape\n(RunStepRecord stays lean); GET /runs/:id/logs is its only reader.\nRETENTION IS UNBOUNDED, DELIBERATELY (review hardening 6): nothing reaps `at`. ~120 bytes per row, written\nONLY by clients that send a key (the dashboard sends none), so single-digit MB per million keyed creates.\nReaping is not free correctness - deleting a mapping RE-ARMS the key, so a later retry starts a second run;\nany future reaper needs a window longer than a client retries, stated as a risk, not a silent cleanup job.",
